        "round_num": 1,
        "expectation_status": "baseline",
        "root_cause": "First measured point. The flat 1-D masked elementwise kernel is correct for all 27 correctness workloads (abs_err=0, rel_err=0) but its latency is additive in grid size rather than bandwidth-bound: 1024x1024 latency is 38.7-38.8 us for fp32/fp16/bf16 alike, i.e. independent of dtype and of bytes moved. Fitting the three measured points (grid=1 -> 1.11 us, grid=32 -> 2.47 us, grid=1024 -> 38.8 us) gives ~1.1 us fixed cost plus ~40 ns per launched program, so per-program scheduling overhead dominates at n=1M while tiny/medium inputs (grid 1-32) already beat the reference by 1.6-2.6x.",
        "perf_gap_analysis": "",
        "next_suggestion": "Raise the per-program tile from BLOCK=1024 to BLOCK=8192 so the 1M-element cases launch 128 programs instead of 1024. If the ~40 ns/program overhead model is correct, the large-input latency should fall from ~38.8 us toward ~6 us, lifting min_speedup and geo_mean; verify that the grid=1 tiny cases do not regress.",
        "optimization_level": "L3_parameter",
